on?-1-icon-chevron" style="font-size: 24px; width: 24px; height: 24px; color: currentcolor;"><span class="path1"></span><span class="path2"></span><span class="path3"></span><span class="path4"></span><span class="path5"></span><span class="path6"></span><span class="path7"></span><span class="path8"></span><span class="path9"></span><span class="path10"></span><span class="path11"></span><span class="path12"></span><span class="path13"></span><span class="path14"></span></i></span></button><div id="accordion-content-_r_3_-why-is-my-brake-light-on?-1" role="region" aria-labelledby="accordion-header-_r_3_-why-is-my-brake-light-on?-1" class="overflow-hidden transition-[height] duration-300 ease-in pb-0 px-0 pb-6" aria-hidden="true" data-qa-id="accordion-details-_r_3_-why-is-my-brake-light-on?-1" style="overflow: hidden; transition: height 0.3s; display: none; height: 0px;"><div class="flex flex-col gap-4"><div class="text-black last:mb-0"><p class="paragraph-md text-action-color-secondary !normal-case">If your vehicle’s brake warning light comes on, you should have your vehicle inspected immediately. The warning light could illuminate for several reasons, including low brake fluid, worn brake pads, leaking brakes, or even a faulty sensor that may affect your antilock braking system.</p><p class="paragraph-md text-action-color-secondary !normal-case"></p></div></div></div></div><div class="border-t-2 last:border-b-2 text-type-standard  border-background-100 border-background-100" data-open="false" data-qa-id="accordion-item-_r_3_-why-does-my-car-and-steering-wheel-shake-when-i-brake?-2"><button aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="accordion-content-_r_3_-why-does-my-car-and-steering-wheel-shake-when-i-brake?-2" id="accordion-header-_r_3_-why-does-my-car-and-steering-wheel-shake-when-i-brake?-2" class="py-4 pr-6 pl-0 cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center w-full border-none text-left gap-6 capitalize bg-transparent paragraph-lg text-type-standard  border-t-bac